title: pip-tools: From Jazzband to PyPA
---
author: Sviatoslav
---
twitter_handle: webknjaz
---
body:

[pip-tools] v7.7.0 is out, and it's the first release we've shipped from
its new home under the [Python Packaging Authority][PyPA]. [Stephen]
usually writes the release notes and I usually ~~procrastinate~~ plan to
write something "later". But a move like this doesn't happen every
decade (well, almost exactly once a decade), so here's my half of the
story.

[pip-tools]: https://pip-tools.pypa.io
[PyPA]: https://www.pypa.io
[Stephen]: https://github.com/sponsors/sirosen

[//]: # (PLUGIN-READ-FULL-POST)

## Nine and a half years of jazz 🎷

[Vincent][nvie] moved pip-tools into [Jazzband] back in [February
2017][into Jazzband]. Jazzband's idea was simple and kind of radical:
anyone who joined got push access, and projects were looked after by the
crowd. It worked surprisingly well for a long time. I became one of the
project leads [in June 2025][webknjaz lead], and Stephen [followed in
August][sirosen lead].

I'll admit something. I used to argue *against* moving pip-tools to the
PyPA. My worry was that it would make it harder for new people to get
involved, since Jazzband gave anybody a way in. That argument stopped
mattering pretty quickly.

[nvie]: https://github.com/sponsors/nvie
[Jazzband]: https://jazzband.co
[into Jazzband]: https://github.com/pypa/pip-tools/issues/447#issuecomment-280703583
[webknjaz lead]: https://github.com/jazzband/help/issues/391#issuecomment-2961416506
[sirosen lead]: https://github.com/jazzband/help/issues/404#issuecomment-3173763162

## The music stops

On March 14, 2026, [Jannis] published [Sunsetting Jazzband] along with a
[wind-down plan]. He gave three reasons:

- the flood of AI-generated PRs made "anyone can push" untenable
- the whole operation depended on a single person
- the rest of the ecosystem had moved on to things like trusted
  publishing

Django projects had [Django Commons] to go to. Projects like pip-tools
didn't have a designated successor, so we had to pick one ourselves.

[Jannis]: https://github.com/sponsors/jezdez
[Sunsetting Jazzband]: https://jazzband.co/news/2026/03/14/sunsetting-jazzband
[wind-down plan]: https://jazzband.co/news/2026/03/14/wind-down-plan
[Django Commons]: https://github.com/django-commons

## Democracy, the mailing list edition 🗳️

I opened ["🎺 Life after Jazzband 🎷"][Life after Jazzband] the same
evening. Stephen and I had met in person the year before and found we
agreed on most things about maintenance, so we didn't need much
discussion. On March 19, I started [a vote on the pypa-committers
list][PyPA vote]. I pitched pip-tools as an early DIY lock file
implementation that Dependabot relies on and that inspired parts of uv.

Then came the bureaucratic hiccup: Stephen couldn't vote. He wasn't on
the mailing list, even though he's a PyPA committer. [Dustin] fixed that
in about five minutes. On April 2, I [declared the vote passed][PyPA vote
result] with fourteen +1s and no objections.

We also checked with the emeritus leads still listed in Jazzband.
[Vincent Philippon][vphilippon], [David Genest][davidovich] and [Albert
Tugushev][atugushev] all gave the move their blessing.
[Vincent's reply][vphilippon reply] is worth reading; it ends with
"many joyful tears". [Hugo] reminded everyone that pip-tools is used to
[release CPython itself][release-tools], no pressure 😅.

[Life after Jazzband]: https://github.com/pypa/pip-tools/discussions/2353
[PyPA vote]: https://mail.python.org/archives/list/pypa-committers@python.org/thread/QOWFH2ZAYYFNIYEEK4PODE56JW5RWCWL/
[PyPA vote result]: https://mail.python.org/archives/list/pypa-committers@python.org/message/RMEHCGGJ6EW7EWYZ77H6RWBRKMZ3GLWO/
[Dustin]: https://github.com/sponsors/di
[vphilippon]: https://github.com/sponsors/vphilippon
[davidovich]: https://github.com/sponsors/davidovich
[atugushev]: https://github.com/sponsors/atugushev
[vphilippon reply]: https://github.com/pypa/pip-tools/discussions/2353#discussioncomment-16296608
[Hugo]: https://github.com/sponsors/hugovk
[release-tools]: https://github.com/python/release-tools

## …and then we waited

Agreeing to move is the easy part. Actually moving required the Jazzband
side to hand things over, and the [wind-down plan] scheduled transfers
for June through December. After a chat at EuroPython, Jannis came back
in October and said, roughly, "let's just do it now". So we did.

## The actual move 🚚

Here's what happened, roughly in order:

- **October 12:** Jannis added Stephen and me as PyPI owners.
- **October 13:** [Pradyun] got temporary admin and transferred the repo
  to `pypa/`, then posted "IDK who all lost access" 😅.
- [Jacob] set up DNS, and the docs now live at
  [pip-tools.pypa.io][pip-tools].
- I reinstalled the GitHub Apps and moved Read the Docs from legacy
  webhooks to its GitHub App. Codecov had somehow decided our default
  branch was one of Stephen's feature branches 🙃.
- A fun gotcha: the `pypi` deployment environment still required
  approval from the `jazzband/pip-tools-leads` team, even though the
  repo was no longer in Jazzband. Apparently GitHub keeps references to
  teams from another org after a transfer. Now it uses PyPA's team.
- Stephen did the bulk of the cleanup in the repo:
  - project links and the switch to the PSF Code of Conduct
  - removing the Jazzband upload step from the release workflow
  - a brand new `SECURITY.md`
  - re-wiring Trusted Publishing

All the bookkeeping is in [jazzband/help#435][Jazzband help issue].
Jannis was extremely thorough on his end, down to reconciling fourteen
upload records nobody but him remembered existed.

[Pradyun]: https://github.com/sponsors/pradyunsg
[Jacob]: https://github.com/sponsors/JacobCoffee
[Jazzband help issue]: https://github.com/jazzband/help/issues/435

## So what changes for you?

Almost nothing:

- GitHub redirects the old URLs.
- `pip install pip-tools` works exactly as before.
- The docs have a new address.

v7.7.0 is also the first real end-to-end test of the new release
pipeline. If you're reading this, it probably worked 🤞.

The interesting changes are in the release itself: Python 3.15 support,
dropping 3.9, a new `pip-tools[stable]` extra and more. Stephen covers
them [in the changelog][changelog]. Go read that instead of my ramblings.

[changelog]: https://pip-tools.pypa.io/en/stable/changelog/

## Thanks 💜

Thank you, Jannis, for a decade of Jazzband and for such a careful
handover. Thanks to Pradyun, Dustin, Jacob and Hugo for doing the
behind-the-scenes work. Thanks to everyone on pypa-committers who voted,
and to the emeritus maintainers for so many years of work.

And thanks, Stephen, for doing most of the heavy lifting, as usual. If
you'd like to keep him going, here's [his sponsors page][Stephen]. Mine
is [right here][webknjaz], too.

See you in the new home! 🖖

[webknjaz]: https://github.com/sponsors/webknjaz

